  "notes": "West leads a spade against 3NT. South should win the opening spade lead as a heart shift from East would not be welcome. South has 2 spade tricks, 2 diamond tricks, and 3 club tricks on top. 9 tricks will be easy unless clubs are 4-0.\n\nSouth can score 5 club tricks even on a 4-0 break if he can decide which defender has 4 clubs. If West has 4 clubs, it is necessary to cash the ♣K first. South can then finesse West out of his ♣J and ♣9.\n\nIf East started with 4 clubs, it is necessary to cash the ♣A or ♣Q first. East can then be finessed for the ♣J.\n\nSouth should play West to be long in clubs and cash the ♣K first. The key is West's lead of the ♠5. The ♠5 is the lowest outstanding spade. As West would lead his fourth best spade, he is marked with a 4 card suit.\n\nIf East had all 4 clubs, West would have to have at least a 5 card suit somewhere. West would have led that suit and not spades.",
